course: dictionary
lesson: 14817
title: Qualifying: earning a place
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Qualify means to pass a test, meet the rules or do well enough to earn a place. Qualifying is its -ing form, and it often describes a test that comes before the big event. «The qualifying race decides who can start the main race.» Here qualifying tells you which race it is.
explain: Look at the words around it to find the meaning. «Only the top three runners in qualifying can go through to the final.» The words top three, go through and final are clues: qualifying is a test that comes first, and you must do well in it to go on.
explain: In sport, qualifying can also be a noun for the practice races that decide who starts where. «Lewis was fastest in qualifying, so he starts at the front.» Fastest and starts at the front are the clues. Swap in your own meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Other rules work the same way. «She had reached the qualifying age, so she could join the club.» Qualifying age means the age you must reach to meet the rules. When the context is not enough, a dictionary will settle it.
fact: Qualifying is the -ing form of qualify.
fact: As an adjective, a qualifying round or race decides who goes through to the next stage.
fact: In sport, qualifying can be a noun: the practice races that decide starting places.
fact: In grammar, a qualifying word limits another word, and in money a qualifying purchase is one that meets the rules.
fact: Context clues such as «final» or «starts at the front» help you work out what qualifying means.
exercise: «The qualifying round decided who could go on to the final.» What does qualifying mean here? | *A test you must do well in to go through | A party to celebrate the final | A round just for children | A quiet, slow game
exercise: «Only the fastest drivers in qualifying start at the front.» What does this tell you about qualifying? | It is the last race of the day | They must wear helmets | They must sing before the race | *It decides who gets a good starting place
exercise: Sam says qualifying means playing a game with friends. Why is Sam wrong? | Qualifying means sleeping early | *Qualifying means trying to earn a place or pass the rules | Qualifying means cooking dinner | Qualifying means winning every race
test: «The qualifying age for the club is ten.» What does qualifying age mean? | A funny number | Age to be allowed in for fun | *The age you must reach to meet the rules | The oldest age in the club
test: «Only the qualifying numbers can go into the sum.» In a maths word problem, what are qualifying numbers? | Numbers that are odd | Numbers written in words | *Numbers that meet the condition | Numbers that are very big
exam: «The qualifying match decides who goes through to the cup final.» What does qualifying mean in this sentence? | A match played at night | A match that is cancelled | *A match that decides who goes through | Friendly and just for fun
